WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.868 --> 00:00:05.610
[LOGO]

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.370
I'm really excited to announce
our last, but absolutely

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:12.870
not the least, keynote speakers,
Roger Peng and Hilary Parker,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.510
the hosts of Not So
Standard Deviations.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.891
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:23.870
If you've never
heard of it before,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.330
really fantastic podcast.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.490
They've never invited me
to be a guest speaker,

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.068
but fortunately, I'm not
bitter about that at all.

00:00:33.068 --> 00:00:35.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.440
But seriously, a very long
time fan of Roger and Hilary.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.430
Roger has been a supporter
of the Tidyverse,

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.790
really before it
was the Tidyverse.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:49.910
And I think his use
of our 2018 keynote

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.890
does a better job of
kind of articulating

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.650
the philosophy and the
benefits of the Tidyverse

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.660
than I've ever managed.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.310
I'm also very fortunate
to call Hilary a friend.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.740
Hilary is a fantastic
data scientist.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.110
I've had a number of really
insightful conversations

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:06.920
with her.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.470
One recent one, which led me
to really understand, like,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.890
one of the things
that I do is design.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.250
But as well as being a data
scientist at Stitch Fix,

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:16.790
Hilary--

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.745
I'm also very lucky that
Hilary has acted as my stylist.

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:22.370
And she actually just picked out
the clothes I'm wearing today.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.346
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:24.346 --> 00:01:27.804
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.630
So I'd like you
to please join me

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.100
in welcoming Hilary and Roger.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.970
OK, so you're going to get
a little behind the scenes

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.840
action here, because the
listeners of our show

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.190
don't see this, but we
have to do this every time.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:53.482
You know when you're dealing
in a high stakes situation

00:01:53.482 --> 00:01:55.170
like this, safety comes first.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.370
Yeah.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:02.290
So we have a checklist that
we go through every time.

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.270
So we're going to go
through the checklist.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.540
OK, Hilary, hard wired?

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.757
ROGER PENG: I guess we
don't really need that.

00:02:08.757 --> 00:02:09.690
VPN turned off?

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:10.523
HILARY PARKER: Yeah.

00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:12.277
My work VPN causes
problems with our--

00:02:12.277 --> 00:02:13.860
ROGER PENG: A number
of problems, yes.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.068
HILARY PARKER: --connections.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.350
ROGER PENG: Wi-Fi turned off?

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.160
HILARY PARKER: No.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.493
ROGER PENG: Oh, actually, I
haven't turned off my Wi-Fi.

00:02:19.493 --> 00:02:22.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.080
OK.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:27.470
Reboot your router?

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:27.970
Yeah.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.860
[LAUGHS] Almost always
my internet is an issue.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.110
ROGER PENG: Verify microphone?

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.777
I think the people
in the back that seem

00:02:34.777 --> 00:02:36.840
to know what they're doing.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:37.670
Verify recording?

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.410
We are recording.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:40.110
All right.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.280
Hard wired.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.397
I'm not recording today.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.230
Files being written?

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.610
I'm not doing that.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.340
HILARY PARKER: We did once
do a whole podcast episode

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:50.395
where we did not record.

00:02:50.395 --> 00:02:51.020
That's right.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:53.437
Yeah, so once that
happened, like never again.

00:02:53.437 --> 00:02:55.270
Guess how that made
it onto the checklist?

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:55.902
Yeah.

00:02:55.902 --> 00:02:59.070
[LAUGHS] We did a
blameless postmortem.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.350
Levels seems to be OK?

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:00.950
Yeah.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:01.450
All right.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.530
And it is close to 5 o'clock.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:04.050
Yeah.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.753
So--

00:03:04.753 --> 00:03:06.133
Verify that my
cat's been feed.

00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:07.050
--you feed your cat?

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:07.550
Yeah.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:12.142
Yeah, she does-- from 4 to 5,
she starts to interrupt things.

00:03:12.142 --> 00:03:14.350
That was more of an issue
when you were in Melbourne.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.110
Yeah.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:19.012
Yeah, so all right, so I
think we're go for launch?

00:03:19.012 --> 00:03:19.970
Yeah, we're launched.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.820
So welcome to the Not
So Standard Deviations

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:23.930
episode 100.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.395
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.070
I'm Roger Peng from the Johns
Hopkins Bloomberg School

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:35.778
of Public Health.

00:03:35.778 --> 00:03:37.950
And I'm here with Hilary
Parker of Stitch Fix.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.340
So this episode is our
first ever live episode.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.620
It's, I guess, ironically not
going to be about R or RStudio.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.190
Yeah.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.950
It's possible we showed
up at the wrong conference.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.695
But I hope you'll enjoy it.

00:03:54.695 --> 00:03:55.570
I think it'll be fun.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.090
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.630
We also did some
prep, unusually.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.773
Usually we kind of show up
and call each other and say,

00:04:03.773 --> 00:04:05.440
I was thinking about
talking about this.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.040
And so this time we actually
have some materials.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:08.540
Yes.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.510
Yeah.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.370
All right, so
there are probably

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.430
a few people in the audience
who haven't heard of

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.137
or heard our podcast,
so we thought

00:04:16.137 --> 00:04:17.720
we'd just talk a
little bit about kind

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:21.700
of why we're here, what we're
doing, why we're doing this.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.390
And so I guess this
started in 2015,

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:28.080
right after the rOpenSci
Unconference here

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.940
in San Francisco, where Hilary
and I kind of caught up.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:32.940
And I sent her an email.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.010
I said, hey--

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.510
I think the subject
of the email was

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:38.590
I want to broadcast your
opinions to the world.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:39.232
Yeah.

00:04:39.232 --> 00:04:41.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.670
Because Hilary
has a few opinions.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.960
And I thought there's nothing
the internet likes more

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.450
than a strongly
opinionated person.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.660
So I sent her an
email, said, hey, I

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.740
think we could do a
data science podcast.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.630
I don't think there's
exactly what I'm

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.255
thinking about out there already
in terms of other data science

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:58.410
podcasts.

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.397
And so do you want to do it?

00:05:00.397 --> 00:05:01.480
And so she got back to me.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.490
She said yes.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.200
And I sent her a wall
of text email saying,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.380
here's what I
think we should do.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.778
And then there was no response.

00:05:08.778 --> 00:05:10.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:11.220
Yeah.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.060
But then--

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.700
--a week later,
I apologized a lot.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:15.450
Yes.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.820
And it was true that my
boss quit that week, so there

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.180
was some reason, but, yeah, it
was mostly the wall of text.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.810
Said yes and then
we just went for it.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.280
Yeah, for five years.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.538
Or almost five years, I
should say, so 99 episodes.

00:05:30.538 --> 00:05:31.330
And so here we are.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:35.598
So we've been talking
about a lot of things

00:05:35.598 --> 00:05:36.640
over the past five years.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.680
And one of the things
that we realized

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.600
as we were preparing
for this keynote

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.780
is that we have a record
of everything we've said.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:43.380
Yeah.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.110
Yeah, so I wanted to make sure
there there's a visual impact

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:46.890
here.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:53.460
But I actually printed out the
transcripts of every episode

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.770
and leafed through them.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:59.520
Because it's kind of rare
that you have the ability

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.860
to see what you were
thinking over a period

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.390
of time like this,
aside from kind

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.710
of, like, a personal journal.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.515
Which I have joked that
the podcast is a little bit

00:06:07.515 --> 00:06:09.140
of a personal journal.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:13.110
But yeah, so we wanted
to look back and see

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.760
what themes were coming up.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.910
And we never set
out with an agenda

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.700
for this, although I kind
of thought you did at first.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.430
But we just wanted to chat.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.070
And then what was surprising
was that we actually got

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.720
some places during these chats.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.170
And so part of the reason
I wanted to look back

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.670
through these
transcripts was to see

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.890
how thoughts evolved over time.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:37.502
So just before we move
on to the next thing,

00:06:37.502 --> 00:06:39.210
I just wanted to
mention, as we were kind

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.280
of leading up to this keynote--

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.350
they asked us to do
this a while back

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.570
and we saw kind of
who else was going up.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:51.870
So for example, JJ's keynote
yesterday, it was amazing.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.780
And a lot-- maybe
coincidentally or not,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.510
touched on a lot of themes
that we had been discussing

00:06:58.510 --> 00:06:59.800
in terms of open source.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:00.360
Yeah.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.443
And for those of
you who are wondering,

00:07:02.443 --> 00:07:04.110
we're not going to
talk about that here.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:06.485
Yeah, we're not going to
talk about open source at all.

00:07:06.485 --> 00:07:09.270
So even though our episodes
have been leading up to that--

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:13.350
and again, we may have had
some information there that

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.140
emboldened those
conversations, but--

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.390
Look out for episode 101.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:20.550
Yeah, episode 101 we'll
get back on track with that.

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.590
Yeah, we wanted
to touch on those.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.700
The keynotes have
been awesome so far.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.730
And especially, I mean,
JJ's was really good.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.790
And we were joking about
the fact that, didn't

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.400
realize what a nerd he was.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.280
Or what, like, a
policy nerd he was.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.060
[LAUGHS]

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.930
I told him it
warmed by heart when

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:45.150
he brought up the case history
of the Delaware Supreme Court.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:47.370
Nothing more riveting than--

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.235
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:48.235 --> 00:07:50.610
Nothing more riveting than a
little bit of corporate case

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:51.193
law, you know?

00:07:51.193 --> 00:07:53.530
[LAUGHS] Exactly.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.470
And it was just so
lucky that someone

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.570
who had so much interest
in public policy,

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.020
but then became a
highly productive coder

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.240
for many years, and then circled
back and brought them together.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:07.540
It's unusual.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.080
And then, yeah,
the second keynote

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.880
with all that data visualization
really spoke to me.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:16.620
Because at Stitch Fix, I
work a lot with outfit data.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.600
There's a lot of imagery
in fashion, right?

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:22.980
And so I was really inspired
by that about different ways

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.960
to visualize the data.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.130
And actually, JJ also
was talking about this--

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.470
the book was called
Shop Class as Soulcraft

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.039
and this idea of
touching the data

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:36.700
and making the data yourself.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.200
And looking through
this transcript,

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:41.289
actually, you had
mentioned Scott Zeger

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:44.270
talking about writing
the data down by hand.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:47.170
And so I thought
that was awesome.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:48.997
That actually comes
from John Tukey,

00:08:48.997 --> 00:08:50.830
who talks about scratching
the numbers down.

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:51.265
Oh, really?

00:08:51.265 --> 00:08:51.765
Yeah.

00:08:51.765 --> 00:08:53.530
Oh, there you go.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:57.375
I have to say, when we saw
that the two people from Google

00:08:57.375 --> 00:08:58.750
were going to talk
at keynote, we

00:08:58.750 --> 00:08:59.810
didn't know what
it was going to be,

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.636
but I was like, oh, that one's
going to be good, actually.

00:09:02.636 --> 00:09:04.020
[LAUGHS] They're going to--

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.550
--be very polished,
very prepared.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:08.110
We're like, let's
do the opposite.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:09.707
Yeah, right?

00:09:09.707 --> 00:09:12.040
Let's run in the opposite
direction of whatever they do.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:15.090
Yeah, that was where the idea
of the live episode came from.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:15.610
Yeah.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.452
And then Jenny's
talk was great, too.

00:09:18.452 --> 00:09:19.660
She always has that amazing--

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:20.602
Yeah.

00:09:20.602 --> 00:09:25.030
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.600
She has an incredible
ability to just see

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.140
that higher level of stuff after
having been in the trenches

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:31.643
for so long.

00:09:31.643 --> 00:09:32.310
Exactly, yeah.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:35.130
Like, going into the detail and
understanding the nitty gritty,

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:38.680
but then being able to zoom out
and create the paradigm for it.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.898
It's not easy, so good work.

00:09:41.898 --> 00:09:43.530
[LAUGHS]

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.138
Don't get too
comfortable, though.

00:09:45.138 --> 00:09:47.430
No, but I feel like this
conference has been awesome.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:52.680
And we have, like, we have
fodder for many episodes

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.176
following up on this.

00:09:54.176 --> 00:09:56.520
And such a great vibe.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:57.770
This is your first--

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:59.100
It's my first rstudio::conf.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:09:59.905
That's right, yeah.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.670
So we went through--

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.670
so one of the ideas that
we had for this keynote

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.545
was to kind of go through
our thought process

00:10:07.545 --> 00:10:09.753
for the last five years,
which was made a lot of user

00:10:09.753 --> 00:10:12.003
by looking at the transcripts,
listening to the audio,

00:10:12.003 --> 00:10:13.810
hearing what we've
been talking about.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:17.650
And as we did that
in preparation,

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.590
we noticed a number of kind
of interesting moments,

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:24.660
of course, and kind
of recurring theme.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:26.520
One of the interesting
moments that we had,

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.062
actually really
early on the podcast,

00:10:28.062 --> 00:10:32.070
was our first and only really
kind of use of profanity.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.470
[LAUGHS]

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.080
And maybe I just want to
ask, maybe do a quick poll--

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.430
who thinks it was me and
who thinks it was Hilary?

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.830
Who thinks it was Hilary?

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:44.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:45.300
Wow.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.715
Who thinks it was me?

00:10:46.715 --> 00:10:48.110
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.740
About 50-50.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:52.055
Let me play you this clip.

00:10:52.055 --> 00:10:54.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.240
JENNY BRYAN (RECORDING): When
people use spread-shit sheets,

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.390
