00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:08.140
and the website went down--

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:11.410
there is a human
tendency to blame

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:14.170
the person who wrote the
code that failed or whatever.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.050
But Etsy focused
a lot on instead

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:19.570
doing this thing called a
blameless postmortem, where

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:22.480
you talk about--

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:24.700
you frame the problem
as though the system

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:27.730
failed the operator, rather
than the operator failing

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:29.080
within a good system.

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.210
And then that opens
up the conversation

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.460
to talk about iterating
on the systems.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:36.820
And saying, OK, this person
went to work this day,

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:38.770
said they wanted
to do a good job.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:41.260
They did not say they wanted
to take down the website.

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:45.340
But then the system allowed
them to take down the website.

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:48.520
And so based on
this kind of idea

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:49.980
of how do we know
what to do, there

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:51.938
were sort of these two
threads that started it.

00:21:51.938 --> 00:21:56.200
One was how do we
decide how to build

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:58.690
the artifact--
like the dashboard

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:00.617
or the email or the
report or whatever.

00:22:00.617 --> 00:22:02.200
And then there was
this kind of, like,

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.370
how do you build a narrative.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:07.630
And so I think like the theme
of the early part of the podcast

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:11.230
really for the first year
was focused on that artifact

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:17.170
question of how do you build a
system that doesn't fail you?

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:21.010
How do you decide
what to do in order

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:25.930
to make an analysis
happen that works for you?

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:30.220
And I was really energized by
connecting these two things

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:31.720
and being like,
well, the way we can

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:36.970
talk about it as a community
is building systems

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:38.320
that help you avoid errors.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:40.990
And articulating what those
errors are, putting cost

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:44.260
with those errors, and then
making design decisions

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:45.500
for your system around that.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:46.000
Yeah.

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:46.330
Yeah.

00:22:46.330 --> 00:22:48.730
A lot of the struggle that
we had was in terms of kind

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:52.040
of identifying the
constraints and identifying

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:54.600
kind of the framing
of this problem

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.890
and otherwise, it's so open.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:01.410
Yeah, and if you don't
define this problem that way

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.400
from kind of more
first principles,

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.410
you end up in these like
nasty language wars.

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.660
Or you get people who are
just saying like, you should

00:23:07.660 --> 00:23:09.100
use this, you should use knitr.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:11.770
And you don't talk about why.

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:15.010
And it's like, OK, when is
it appropriate to use knitr?

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:17.050
If you want to avoid
these types of errors,

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:19.580
you don't want to
update your data,

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:21.880
but have your report be stale.

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:22.820
That's a huge one.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:26.630
And so being able to talk about
the system that way, I think,

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:31.090
was an important part of this
kind of thread of thought.

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:33.610
And so again, we kind
of separated out that.

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:35.560
And I personally
focus on that a lot.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:39.880
I mean, we both did, but I think
that was in my head because

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:41.110
