00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:01.270
And I've studied music theory.

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:03.260
I've done a lot of
that kind of work.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:06.490
And I just started thinking
that there kind of were

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:10.240
some connections between what
we do as data analysts and kind

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:11.902
of how we think about music.

00:26:11.902 --> 00:26:14.110
And this is just a little
bit of what we talked about

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:15.604
in that episode.

00:26:15.604 --> 00:26:17.113
Oops.

00:26:17.113 --> 00:26:19.280
ROGER PENG (RECORDING): The
more I thought about it,

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:22.460
the more I felt like there
were kind of connections

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:25.070
between what we do in data
analysis-- or not what we do,

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:29.720
what we need in data analysis
and kind of music theory,

00:26:29.720 --> 00:26:30.410
right?

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:33.340
And so the basic idea is that--

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:38.385
music is considered to be a
very kind of subjective area,

00:26:38.385 --> 00:26:40.010
in terms of like what
you think is good

00:26:40.010 --> 00:26:42.350
and what you think
is not good, right?

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:45.320
But that said, most of us have
a sense of what sounds good

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:47.490
and what doesn't sound good.

00:26:47.490 --> 00:26:47.990
Right.

00:26:47.990 --> 00:26:50.780
I think I talked a lot about
there is a theory of harmony,

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:53.363
there is a theory of
melody, in terms of how

00:26:53.363 --> 00:26:54.530
to construct certain things.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:59.420
And those rules don't
produce the best music,

00:26:59.420 --> 00:27:04.070
but they do allow us to describe
and to kind of summarize

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.980
what it is that people
seem to like about music.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:10.280
And it's not an absolute theory.

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:11.900
It's a very kind
of based in culture

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:16.030
and based in people's
kind of collective ideas,

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:18.740
but there is a theoretical
structure there.

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:21.860
And there's some
science to it.

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:24.550
I mean, there's literally
wave properties.

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:26.360
There is a little
physics involved, yeah.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:29.150
Yeah, and then there's
kind of like a psychology

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:30.920
or something--

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:34.610
like wanting to hear chords
resolved, for example.

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:35.778
I'm not totally sure what--

00:27:35.778 --> 00:27:36.320
I don't know.

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:39.144
I mean, I'm like, continue
this thought please.

00:27:39.144 --> 00:27:40.186
I don't know much about--

00:27:40.186 --> 00:27:40.686
[LAUGHS]

00:27:40.686 --> 00:27:43.550
So I got very deep
in the weeds of this,

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:45.580
reading Arnold
Schoenberg's Harmonielehre.

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:47.990
And the way he
summarizes it, I think,

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.210
the best in the sense
that the theory doesn't

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:51.530
say what's right.

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:54.320
The theory says
what's commonly done.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:57.380
And so the idea that
the five chord resolves

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:59.540
to the one chord, that's
not because it's right,

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:01.148
it's just because
that seems to be

00:28:01.148 --> 00:28:02.690
what everyone does
and it seems to be

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:05.900
kind of not too bad of an idea.

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:06.530
Yeah.

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:08.988
But if you don't resolve the
five chord to the one chord,

00:28:08.988 --> 00:28:10.777
it's not, like, a
theoretical violation.

00:28:10.777 --> 00:28:11.985
I'm just laughing because--

00:28:11.985 --> 00:28:12.657
Uh-oh.

00:28:12.657 --> 00:28:15.530
[LAUGHS] Well, I'm just
thinking about that second

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:19.160
lecture or the second keynote,
where he got like really

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:20.903
defensive about pie charts.

00:28:20.903 --> 00:28:22.070
And I think that's exactly--

00:28:22.070 --> 00:28:25.330
it's what people are doing.

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:26.680
You can't argue with that.

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:27.597
It is commonly done.

00:28:27.597 --> 00:28:31.370
Yeah, and it
seems to work for--

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:32.080
I'm with him.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:33.840
Like, for certain things.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:35.450
This is what happens
at our podcast.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:35.955
We get sidetracked--

00:28:35.955 --> 00:28:36.470
We get sidetracked.

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:36.970
[LAUGHS]

