00:31:17.465 --> 00:31:18.590
What I was trying to say.

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:19.673
--we were talking about.

00:31:19.673 --> 00:31:20.750
Yeah.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.417
HILARY PARKER
(RECORDING): Data analysis

00:31:22.417 --> 00:31:26.690
to influence decision making
has to span both the world where

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:28.640
third person perspective
and kind of this,

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:33.200
like, scientific truth world is
and then also that first person

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:38.180
eliciting belief in another
human, when you can't really

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:41.750
observe what will make that
person start to believe

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:44.960
your results or trust it.

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:47.870
So anyway, I thought
it added clar--

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:55.150
to me, it added some clarity of
the complexity of what we do.

00:31:55.150 --> 00:32:01.250
Yeah, so to set that up,
the idea was essentially that

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:03.330
neuroscientists, they
can study-- in this case,

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:05.705
they're talking about studying
people who are meditating.

00:32:05.705 --> 00:32:10.070
And you can't exactly
empirically observe,

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:12.148
someone's in a meditative state.

00:32:12.148 --> 00:32:13.940
You can measure their
brainwaves and stuff,

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.430
but ultimately, you have to
rely on the person telling you

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:18.020
that they're in
that state, so you

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:21.380
can correlate the brainwaves
with them having that state.

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:23.450
And so to me, it
was kind of similar,

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:27.530
where in order for an
analysis to be successful,

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:29.730
someone has to be, like,
I get it now, you know?

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:30.980
They have to tell you it's--

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:33.170
Yeah, they have to tell
you that they get it.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.260
And you can't observe it.

00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:37.760
I mean, you can obviously see
their face change or whatever,

00:32:37.760 --> 00:32:41.420
but they're having a
first-person experience.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:43.550
And so I was just
teasing apart like,

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:46.087
oh, yeah, you have to
use scientific methods

00:32:46.087 --> 00:32:47.420
and be in this world of science.

00:32:47.420 --> 00:32:50.030
But then also you have
to tap into this kind

00:32:50.030 --> 00:32:55.010
of deeply empathetic thing of
seeing where their mind is.

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.284
And that's hard.

00:32:57.284 --> 00:32:57.784
[LAUGHS]

00:32:57.784 --> 00:32:59.310
I think I get it now.

00:32:59.310 --> 00:33:02.590
Yeah, yeah, good.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.340
Did we have other clips
from this episode?

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:05.030
I don't think so.

00:33:05.030 --> 00:33:05.530
No.

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:07.190
Yeah.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:08.470
Another thing --

00:33:08.470 --> 00:33:10.220
I think we might have
a clip for this one.

00:33:10.220 --> 00:33:12.620
I was talking about how
blameless postmortems--

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:14.120
around the same
time, too, I started

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.460
to see how blameless
postmortems were--

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:18.090
well, why don't you
just play the clip?

00:33:18.090 --> 00:33:20.257
HILARY PARKER (RECORDING):
I think the biggest thing

00:33:20.257 --> 00:33:25.520
I realized sort of almost right
after was that in some ways

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:33.740
the sort of blameless postmortem
stuff is a design process.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:39.230
And it's a way of almost making
a system to force everyone

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:40.310
to be empathetic.

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:44.480
[LAUGHS] Yeah, because the
point of blameless postmortem

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:50.390
is essentially to say,
here, listen to the problem.

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:51.770
Don't blame the user.

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:54.515
Try to put yourself
in their shoes.

00:33:54.515 --> 00:33:55.640
They essentially force you.

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:58.640
Like, you can't
use you statements.

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:02.510
There's a lot of
rules of engagement.

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:06.357
And so it all kind of
started coming together.

00:34:06.357 --> 00:34:07.190
We're on the path.

00:34:07.190 --> 00:34:08.820
We're on the path, yeah.

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:10.805
And the path
ends at episode 63.

00:34:10.805 --> 00:34:13.534
Or at least the first
milestone, which

00:34:13.534 --> 00:34:15.659
was the start-- some of
you, if you were listeners,

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:19.400
will remember the
seven part book club

00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:22.760
that we had for Nigel
Cross's Design Thinking.

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.760
Which some liked and some
thought was too long.

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:26.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:27.437
Oh.

00:34:27.437 --> 00:34:28.520
I don't want to hear that.

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:31.300
But we'll talk
about feedback later.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:33.290
Yeah.

00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:35.360
Yeah, so I'll
play the clip here.

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:37.380
Just so you know the
name of the clip.

00:34:37.380 --> 00:34:39.380
Oh, and this clip
also really exemplifies

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:41.659
the podcast for people
who don't listen,

00:34:41.659 --> 00:34:45.409
where it takes us a little
while to get to the point.

00:34:45.409 --> 00:34:48.000
Yes, in case you haven't
already figured that out.

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:49.940
Yeah.

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:52.875
The name of this file
is all caps texting.

00:34:52.875 --> 00:34:54.659
All right.

00:34:54.659 --> 00:34:58.626
Oops, that's not
what I was meant to do.

00:34:58.626 --> 00:34:59.126
Here we go.

00:34:59.126 --> 00:35:00.501
ROGER PENG
(RECORDING): And all I

00:35:00.501 --> 00:35:02.360
know is I got these
messages that are,

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:06.590
like, I guess you took a picture
of the Kindle with your phone

00:35:06.590 --> 00:35:08.924
and then texted me the picture?

00:35:08.924 --> 00:35:09.812
Is that right?

00:35:09.812 --> 00:35:12.440
HILARY PARKER (RECORDING):
[LAUGHS] That is correct.

00:35:12.440 --> 00:35:13.857
ROGER PENG
(RECORDING): And so I'm

00:35:13.857 --> 00:35:17.240
like reading your
photo of a Kindle.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:17.883
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:17.883 --> 00:35:19.550
HILARY PARKER
(RECORDING): Well, can you

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:21.020
take a screenshot of a Kindle?

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:21.800
I feel like--

00:35:21.800 --> 00:35:23.100
ROGER PENG (RECORDING):
I've never owned one,

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:23.888
so I'm not sure.

00:35:23.888 --> 00:35:25.430
HILARY PARKER
(RECORDING): Oh, you're

00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:27.230
just, like, tablet all the way.

00:35:27.230 --> 00:35:29.630
ROGER PENG (RECORDING):
Definitely.

00:35:29.630 --> 00:35:31.480
So then I'm reading
these screens--

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:32.480
they're not screenshots.

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:34.002
They're photos of a screen.

00:35:34.002 --> 00:35:35.960
HILARY PARKER (RECORDING):
Well, no, OK, photos

00:35:35.960 --> 00:35:38.290
of an eInk screen.

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:38.810
Yeah.

00:35:38.810 --> 00:35:41.090
I do have a moment
of pain when I see

00:35:41.090 --> 00:35:43.843
a photo of a computer screen.

00:35:43.843 --> 00:35:45.260
ROGER PENG
(RECORDING): And then I

00:35:45.260 --> 00:35:48.530
think what ensued was
a series of like all

00:35:48.530 --> 00:35:51.797
caps exchanges between you and
me that are like, oh, my god,

00:35:51.797 --> 00:35:53.630
I can't believe-- this
is exactly what we're

00:35:53.630 --> 00:35:54.860
talking about.

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:56.318
HILARY PARKER
(RECORDING): Exactly.

00:35:56.318 --> 00:35:59.270
Yeah, no, it was like,
oh, this articulates

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:03.020
what we've been
trying to say in,

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:06.500
like, a few sentences
versus you've

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.630
written a series
of long blog posts.

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:10.520
I mean, I think that
they're still valuable.

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:13.590
ROGER PENG (RECORDING):
But not good concise.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:17.750
Yeah, so I feel this
is was a moment where

00:36:17.750 --> 00:36:20.150
we were talking about these--

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:22.520
I guess the book's called
Designerly Ways of Knowing?

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:23.110
Yeah.

00:36:23.110 --> 00:36:25.850
And we were kind
of reading it.

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:27.710
And she's sending me
photos of the book.

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:29.570
And I'm reading the
book along with her.

00:36:29.570 --> 00:36:31.570
And I'm like, send
me the next page.

00:36:31.570 --> 00:36:32.960
Yeah.

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:33.680
And it was like--

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:37.610
so this was a book essentially
about the theory of--

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:40.790
academic theory around
design and design work.

00:36:40.790 --> 00:36:43.430
And it was just, like, to
us, it was this huge aha

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:47.360
moment of like, oh, it's all
starting to really make sense.

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:51.713
And what was making sense is
that the way the designers

00:36:51.713 --> 00:36:53.630
work and the way that
they structure the work,

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:55.672
the way they talk about
it, you could essentially

00:36:55.672 --> 00:36:58.990
swap in the word data analysis
and it was the same thing.

00:36:58.990 --> 00:37:00.290
In so many ways, yeah.

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:01.720
Yeah, in so many ways.

00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:04.880
Like, the way that designers
talk about getting briefs,

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:07.580
where it's like, oh,
you get a design brief

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:10.130
and then you don't just--

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:12.920
as a designer, you don't just
do what the person asked you,

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:14.870
because that will
be like a subpar--

00:37:14.870 --> 00:37:16.910
the person doesn't
know what they want.

00:37:16.910 --> 00:37:19.280
They have figured out
some way to articulate it,

00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:23.630
but your job as a designer
is to zoom out and identify

00:37:23.630 --> 00:37:25.700
what they actually
need and then make

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.230
the best solution about that.

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:29.600
But they're going to communicate
what they need via what

00:37:29.600 --> 00:37:30.975
they think the solution is.

00:37:30.975 --> 00:37:31.850
And that's the same--

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:34.820
I mean, I think about that all
the time with, like, someone

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:36.112
asks you for a number.

00:37:36.112 --> 00:37:37.820
They're like, can you
get me this number?

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:40.040
And then as a data
analyst, you have

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:42.290
to zoom out and be like,
no, OK, what problem are you

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:42.993
trying to solve?

00:37:42.993 --> 00:37:44.910
Why don't you catch me
up on what you're doing

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:45.860
and maybe I'm
going to solve this

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:46.880
in a totally different way?

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:48.050
Some of my
collaborators, I think,

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:49.842
would want me to just
give them the number.

00:37:49.842 --> 00:37:50.690
I know, yeah.

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:52.192
That's OK, though.

00:37:52.192 --> 00:37:53.900
They don't stay
collaborators for long.

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:54.636
Yeah.

00:37:54.636 --> 00:37:56.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:56.380 --> 00:37:59.340
That sounded way darker
than I meant it to.

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:00.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:00.150 --> 00:38:01.400
I just stop working with them.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:03.580
It's not like-- yeah.

00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:05.840
You don't want that.

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:09.553
So that's kind of the
end of our audio clips.

00:38:09.553 --> 00:38:11.970
But essentially, we got to
this place where it's like, OK,

00:38:11.970 --> 00:38:15.860
data analysis is a type
of essentially either

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:18.110
an independent thing or a
type of design thinking,

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:20.930
where it's like, the answer
to this student's question

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:25.970
is essentially adopt this
whole other way of approaching

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:28.040
the world that's totally
different than science.

00:38:28.040 --> 00:38:31.010
And we haven't totally--

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:35.000
as a field, we haven't totally
addressed that difference.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.550
I kind of wonder about what
I would have told that student

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:40.490
now, more than 10 years later.

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:42.870
And the way I kind
of think about it

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:45.440
is that when you go outside,
you don't see a data

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:47.870
analysis walking around, right?

00:38:47.870 --> 00:38:52.430
It doesn't naturally
occur like a tree or--

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:53.600
what else naturally occurs--

00:38:53.600 --> 00:38:54.100
a rock.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:59.830
I only know about trees.

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:01.430
So if it doesn't
naturally occur,

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:02.942
it has to be built
by someone or it

00:39:02.942 --> 00:39:05.150
has to be designed by someone
and it has to be built.

00:39:05.150 --> 00:39:09.240
And so why shouldn't we
use the same ideas there

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:12.740
for a data analysis as you
might for a chair or a bridge

00:39:12.740 --> 00:39:13.353
or whatever.

00:39:13.353 --> 00:39:14.270
That's a good point.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:16.550
Yeah, I mean, same
with the music, too,

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:21.700
where obviously noises exist,
but crafting the noises

00:39:21.700 --> 00:39:25.040
in a way that's pleasing is, in
many ways, even though it's R,

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:26.490
it's also design.

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:26.990
Yeah.

00:39:26.990 --> 00:39:29.790
And yeah, no, it's a
good way of putting it.

00:39:29.790 --> 00:39:31.790
Like, essentially you're
constructing something.

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:34.100
And then I also think
what's interesting is

00:39:34.100 --> 00:39:37.670
that whether you're building
a production machine learning

00:39:37.670 --> 00:39:40.700
pipeline or you're
building an analysis,

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:43.820
the tools are different, the
types of testing you'll do

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:45.878
is different, kind of the
technical requirements

00:39:45.878 --> 00:39:47.420
are different, but
ultimately, you're

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:49.760
doing the same thing,
which is you're

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:52.370
creating something that's going
to do something for someone.

00:39:52.370 --> 00:39:55.160
[LAUGHS] Like, either it's going
to be a recommender system that

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:57.670
creates recommendations
for a website

00:39:57.670 --> 00:40:01.270
or it's this analysis
that some sort of person

00:40:01.270 --> 00:40:03.720
is going to consume and make
a decision based on that.

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:05.165
Might just be a PDF document.

00:40:05.165 --> 00:40:06.040
Yeah, it could be--

00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:09.370
I mean, literally even
an email with one number

00:40:09.370 --> 00:40:11.230
in it is still--

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:14.470
you have to decide like,
OK, this problem can

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:16.900
be sufficiently
addressed with an email

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:19.810
and that person doesn't
need much context.

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:21.880
And therefore, if I just
send them the email,

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:24.950
that will be enough to
convince them to do something.

00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:25.480
Yeah.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:30.310
And so that put us on a really
interesting path of like,

00:40:30.310 --> 00:40:34.270
OK, so if we think that data
science is a type of design,

00:40:34.270 --> 00:40:37.720
then how do designers work?

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:39.880
If you look at architects,
how are they trained?

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:42.850
If you look at other
designers, how do they

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.800
work in companies, et cetera?

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:49.960
And so one of the things in this
Design Thinking book by Nigel

00:40:49.960 --> 00:40:52.150
Cross is talking a
lot about, you just

00:40:52.150 --> 00:40:54.363
have to do it over and over.

00:40:54.363 --> 00:40:56.530
And you're going to get
better every time you do it.

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:58.822
You're going to go through
different thought processes.

00:40:58.822 --> 00:41:02.590
But you have to literally
exercise the part of your brain

00:41:02.590 --> 00:41:04.930
that does this
constructive thinking,

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:07.795
rather than like the
deductive thinking of science.

00:41:10.600 --> 00:41:12.040
OK, so in data
science, we kind of

00:41:12.040 --> 00:41:14.890
don't do that apprenticeship
model so much.

00:41:14.890 --> 00:41:17.928
I mean, I think we
wish we didn't have to I

00:41:17.928 --> 00:41:19.220
think is what it comes down to.

00:41:19.220 --> 00:41:21.790
Yeah, like, we wish we
could just teach the stuff

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:22.630
and be done with it.

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:25.660
Because it's a lot of work
to go through someone's work

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:27.560
and tell them if
it's working or not.

00:41:27.560 --> 00:41:29.590
But ultimately, if
we kind of accept

00:41:29.590 --> 00:41:32.350
that this is a type of
design and construction,

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:36.360
then you need to be
able to practice it.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:39.580
So we did some cool
stuff with that where--

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:44.080
kind of very different than
the types of challenges

00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:46.570
you'd do with Kaggle and
stuff, where they're like,

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:47.660
here's a data set.

00:41:47.660 --> 00:41:48.280
Analyze it.

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:49.810
Instead it was
like, OK, let's say

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:52.030
you had to build your
system end-to-end.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:54.340
You needed to answer
some question.

00:41:54.340 --> 00:41:55.270
We did commute times.

00:41:55.270 --> 00:41:55.770
[LAUGHS]

00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:56.278
Right.

00:41:56.278 --> 00:41:57.820
We talked about--
well, your commute.

00:41:57.820 --> 00:41:58.612
Yeah, my commute.

00:41:58.612 --> 00:42:00.670
So it was, in San
Francisco, I want

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:03.490
to know exactly how many
minutes and the variance

00:42:03.490 --> 00:42:06.160
in those minutes for
different commute methods

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:08.340
so I can know the
last possible minute

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:11.590
that I can leave my apartment
to make it to a meeting.

00:42:11.590 --> 00:42:15.760
And so it's like, OK,
how would you solve that?

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:18.850
How would you get the data
to solve that problem?

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:20.080
How would you store it?

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:21.350
How would you access it?

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:22.398
How would you analyze it?

00:42:22.398 --> 00:42:23.440
How would you display it?

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:24.430
And then how
would you model it?

00:42:24.430 --> 00:42:25.330
What are the fixed effects?

00:42:25.330 --> 00:42:26.350
What are the random effects?

00:42:26.350 --> 00:42:26.860
Exactly.

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:28.965
You know, it's
amazing how just

00:42:28.965 --> 00:42:31.210
a simple kind of
formulation of a problem

00:42:31.210 --> 00:42:36.370
can bring in every single
aspect of design, of analysis,

00:42:36.370 --> 00:42:37.780
presentation, communication.

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:39.250
Yeah, exactly.

00:42:39.250 --> 00:42:41.440
Like, you're doing
the whole thing.

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:43.120
And what I like
about it, too, is

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:45.040
that the things
like how you store

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:50.840
the data is sort of on the same
par as what models you choose.

00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:52.090
All of those things are equal.

00:42:52.090 --> 00:42:56.740
You have to end up spending
time on all of it, instead of--

00:42:56.740 --> 00:42:57.760
I feel like as a field--

00:42:57.760 --> 00:42:59.302
and what I like
about this conference

00:42:59.302 --> 00:43:01.240
is that, less so
here, but as a field

00:43:01.240 --> 00:43:02.950
we focus so much
just on the methods.

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:07.570
And that really bothers me, in
case that hasn't been clear.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:10.210
And actually, I was talking
with someone last night

00:43:10.210 --> 00:43:14.530
about that, where I'm like,
you know in some ways--

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:19.390
the way that normal data science
conference are structured,

00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:22.060
it's like everyone wants
to talk about the best

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:23.980
method, the latest thing.

00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:26.320
And literally, in
that system, you're

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:29.320
motivated to have bad
data, because it gives you

00:43:29.320 --> 00:43:32.190
more opportunity to
do fancier models.

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:35.740
And so any system
where it's like, OK,

00:43:35.740 --> 00:43:38.140
this person could work
on worse and worse data

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:40.060
and be equally
happy, because they

00:43:40.060 --> 00:43:42.160
get to do all these
fancy things they learned

00:43:42.160 --> 00:43:46.210
at this conference, is like,
that's not solving the problem.

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:49.220
That's choosing not
to solve the problem.

00:43:49.220 --> 00:43:51.640
So by focusing on the whole
thing, it's like, no, OK,

00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:55.240
can you hone in on exactly what
data you need, rather than just

00:43:55.240 --> 00:43:59.350
making do with what has
fallen into your lap one

00:43:59.350 --> 00:44:00.172
way or another?

00:44:00.172 --> 00:44:02.380
I think one of the hardest
things to do in general,

00:44:02.380 --> 00:44:07.810
but also in data sciences
is to kind of pull back

00:44:07.810 --> 00:44:10.320
from trying to maximize
in a single dimension.

00:44:10.320 --> 00:44:10.820
Yeah.

00:44:10.820 --> 00:44:13.660
And to me, the
way I interpreted--

00:44:13.660 --> 00:44:17.730
and even JJ's talk is like,
the way that corporations are

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:20.200
structured is they
maximize on one dimension.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:21.070
Yeah.

00:44:21.070 --> 00:44:22.420
And I think it's hard--

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:25.653
that can end up with some
good and then some bad.

00:44:25.653 --> 00:44:27.070
I think in data
science, it's very

00:44:27.070 --> 00:44:29.070
tempting to kind of go
for the optimal approach,

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:31.990
go for the optimal method,
go for the best prediction.

00:44:31.990 --> 00:44:34.540
But there are other elements,
there's other stakeholders,

00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:36.490
there's other
trade-offs to be made.

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:41.500
I really like that,
because in the second talk,

00:44:41.500 --> 00:44:43.590
she just touched it
briefly at the end

00:44:43.590 --> 00:44:46.690
that it was like,
what are UX problems

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:48.910
and what are designer problems?

00:44:48.910 --> 00:44:51.430
And then the one that was
like, this is actually a design

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:53.330
problem was the loss function.

00:44:53.330 --> 00:44:56.050
Like, it seems like it's
just a data science problem,

00:44:56.050 --> 00:44:57.900
but that's actually
the core user

00:44:57.900 --> 00:44:59.700
experience is what
loss function you use

00:44:59.700 --> 00:45:02.050
and what system are
you actually building.

00:45:02.050 --> 00:45:04.110
And what I like about
that is that it's

00:45:04.110 --> 00:45:05.610
the same with the
corporation thing.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:09.120
It's like, what was the loss
function before versus--

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:12.360
like, change the KPIs basically.

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:14.370
KPI, key performance indicator.

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:15.600
I don't deal with KPIs.

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:16.100
What?

00:45:16.100 --> 00:45:16.980
I don't deal with KPIs.

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:18.355
Yeah, you don't
deal with KPIs.

00:45:18.355 --> 00:45:19.362
Not yet, at least.

00:45:19.362 --> 00:45:21.820
Yeah, like the way I've been
talking about that at Stitch

00:45:21.820 --> 00:45:26.850
Fix is like, yeah, rather than
just optimizing for sales,

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:29.760
how can we construct
new KPIs that might lead

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.040
to different user experiences?

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:32.910
Part of me, I feel like
it'd be great if we could move

00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:35.550
to a place where instead of
saying this is the best thing,

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:38.760
we could say, I really
appreciate this set

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:39.570
of trade-offs.

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:41.340
Yeah, yeah.

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:42.220
I like that.

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:42.845
I don't know.

00:45:42.845 --> 00:45:44.610
But it doesn't quite--

00:45:44.610 --> 00:45:46.990
doesn't sound as nice, I guess.

00:45:46.990 --> 00:45:47.700
I don't know.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:48.660
I think it sounds--

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:49.160
OK.

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:49.670
Anyway.

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:51.818
I know I have one
person with me here.

00:45:51.818 --> 00:45:54.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:57.480
One other thing that-- kind
of the end of this timeline,

00:45:57.480 --> 00:45:59.100
this has just been briefly.

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:02.340
But essentially by
getting to this place,

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:07.290
now it opens up a whole other
set of fields to look into,

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:12.210
which is everything around
creativity, so like all of--

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:14.460
the book that we're
mentioning here

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:16.020
is called The Creative Curve.

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:18.990
And we had a one episode
book club on that.

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:20.470
We learned our lesson.

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:21.135
Yeah.

00:46:21.135 --> 00:46:23.845
[LAUGHS] I was actually about to
suggest that we should do more.

00:46:23.845 --> 00:46:25.470
I was like, let's
retroactively go back

00:46:25.470 --> 00:46:27.178
and do, like, three
episodes on that one.

00:46:27.178 --> 00:46:28.530
Anyway.

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:32.560
But the idea with that was that
it was this guy, Allen Gannett,

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:36.270
who essentially empirically
studied creative people

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:39.640
and how they operate.

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:41.077
And I thought it was awesome.

00:46:41.077 --> 00:46:42.160
I mean, I really liked it.

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:44.702
It really made me feel like,
oh, I can apply these principles

00:46:44.702 --> 00:46:46.650
to my data science work.

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:48.780
And the principles were--

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:50.447
let's make sure--
[LAUGHS] I don't think

00:46:50.447 --> 00:46:51.572
I did ever write that down.

00:46:51.572 --> 00:46:52.410
Consumption.

00:46:52.410 --> 00:46:54.240
Yeah, so there
was consumption.

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:58.243
So creative people
will frequent--

00:46:58.243 --> 00:46:59.910
well, this is why I'm
confused with you.

00:46:59.910 --> 00:47:03.270
So people who are
engaged in movie making,

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:07.302
they watch movies,
like, 20% of the time.

00:47:07.302 --> 00:47:09.010
You're not like that
for whatever reason.

00:47:09.010 --> 00:47:10.302
That's like a difference story.

00:47:10.302 --> 00:47:14.070
[LAUGHS] But just again,
from like empirically

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:17.160
looking at people who are
in these creative fields,

00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:19.440
consuming other people's
work is a big part of it.

00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:20.700
And you digest it.

00:47:20.700 --> 00:47:22.610
You think about it.

00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:23.950
And you iterate on it.

00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:25.620
And I think that aspect--

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:28.030
I mean, if you want to write,
you read a lot of books.

00:47:28.030 --> 00:47:30.700
If you want to write music,
you listen a lot of music.

00:47:30.700 --> 00:47:32.950
One of the aspects that's
typical about data analysis,

00:47:32.950 --> 00:47:35.900
it's not always easy to
read a lot of data analysis,

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:37.220
because they're not out there.

00:47:37.220 --> 00:47:38.970
Yeah, especially in
the corporate world,

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:41.790
like data science specifically.

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:43.290
If you have a big
team, you can read

00:47:43.290 --> 00:47:45.990
what other people are doing,
but otherwise, basically only

00:47:45.990 --> 00:47:47.060
at conferences.

00:47:47.060 --> 00:47:48.490
Or David Robinson's
livestream.

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:48.990
Yeah.

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:51.000
[LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:53.435
It's hard to consume
a lot of data analysis,

00:47:53.435 --> 00:47:54.810
I think is what
it comes down to.

00:47:54.810 --> 00:47:57.150
I think that's a
key step to becoming

00:47:57.150 --> 00:47:58.200
an expert in something.

00:47:58.200 --> 00:48:01.300
Yeah, that was a
great point, yeah.

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:01.830
Iteration.

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:04.110
Iteration, yeah so--

00:48:04.110 --> 00:48:05.350
is their imitation, too?

00:48:05.350 --> 00:48:05.850
Iteration.

00:48:05.850 --> 00:48:06.420
Iteration.

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:10.620
Yeah, so iteration is this
idea that you just keep going.

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:11.580
You keep trying things.

00:48:11.580 --> 00:48:13.080
You do it over and over.

00:48:13.080 --> 00:48:15.570
And again, that kind of
is touched in the design

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:17.730
literature, too,
where it's just like,

00:48:17.730 --> 00:48:20.280
you've gotta keep
doing things and ideas

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:22.980
are going to evolve over time.

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:26.130
And part of why we wanted
to go through those clips

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:31.560
and put up this timeline is
that even the creativity that we

00:48:31.560 --> 00:48:35.130
got from this podcast, that
I totally wasn't expecting--

00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:36.880
like, it took us four years.

00:48:36.880 --> 00:48:39.247
Like, this timeline
is over four years.

00:48:39.247 --> 00:48:40.580
Yeah, timeline's not to scale.

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:42.280
Yeah, it's definitely
not to scale.

00:48:42.280 --> 00:48:45.300
And so it's like,
the number of ways

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:48.780
that we attack this problem
and thought about it was a lot.

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:53.220
And it never felt like
it was work, necessarily.

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.565
It was just, like,
iterating and going on.

00:48:55.565 --> 00:48:56.940
And then there's
the community.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:48:58.500
Yeah, so the community one.

00:48:58.500 --> 00:49:00.430
Another one is
creative communities.

00:49:00.430 --> 00:49:03.600
And so you look at artists
and Andy Warhol, right,

00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:06.450
had the factory, I
think, where it was just

00:49:06.450 --> 00:49:10.840
a bunch of artists in a loft
painting, giving feedback,

00:49:10.840 --> 00:49:11.370
everything.

00:49:11.370 --> 00:49:13.500
And so with that,
essentially it's

00:49:13.500 --> 00:49:16.070
like surrounding yourself
with other creative people

00:49:16.070 --> 00:49:18.110
helps help you be creative.

00:49:18.110 --> 00:49:22.890
And so that's kind of like,
I feel like my sitcom happy

00:49:22.890 --> 00:49:23.700
ending here.

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:26.280
It's, like, coming to
conferences like this,

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:28.140
they're fun, they're energizing.

00:49:28.140 --> 00:49:32.220
And I do think they kind of
embody this creative community,

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:33.990
especially because
so many people are

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:36.040
isolated as data scientists.

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:39.960
Like, not every company
has 100 data scientists.

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.280
Most companies don't.

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:43.920
And so being able to get
together and bounce ideas

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:47.850
off and see what other
people are doing--

00:49:47.850 --> 00:49:50.540
I guess what I'm trying to
say is that is the work.

00:49:50.540 --> 00:49:54.330
It's not like this
isn't productive,

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:55.620
if that makes sense.

00:49:55.620 --> 00:49:58.790
If there was one agenda
item for starting the podcast,

00:49:58.790 --> 00:50:02.940
I think part of it was
to produce that community

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:06.330
for people who may be sitting
in a department somewhere

00:50:06.330 --> 00:50:09.190
or in a company
somewhere by themselves.

00:50:09.190 --> 00:50:11.465
They're forced to be
the lone data analyst.

00:50:11.465 --> 00:50:13.473
And they don't have
anyone to talk to

00:50:13.473 --> 00:50:15.390
or they don't have the
ability to kind of hear

00:50:15.390 --> 00:50:16.770
what other people are
working on and how they're

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:18.270
approaching things like that.

00:50:18.270 --> 00:50:21.090
Yeah, and that's actually--
that's some of the most

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:24.420
meaningful feedback I hear is
when people are like, oh, yeah,

00:50:24.420 --> 00:50:27.900
I'm like alone and it's really
nice to be able to hear how

00:50:27.900 --> 00:50:29.060
other people are working.

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:32.090
It's just like, yeah, that
makes me feel really good.

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:33.300
And then feedback.

00:50:33.300 --> 00:50:34.536
Feedback, yeah.

00:50:34.536 --> 00:50:36.630
[LAUGHS] So feedback.

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:42.180
And this one is definitely
hard, where a big part of--

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:43.860
in this kind of
Creative Curve book

00:50:43.860 --> 00:50:47.400
and just in general
design, you actively

00:50:47.400 --> 00:50:50.100
are soliciting user feedback
all the time, right?

00:50:50.100 --> 00:50:51.910
And that's a huge part of it.

00:50:51.910 --> 00:50:56.520
And you cannot be attached
to what you've produced,

00:50:56.520 --> 00:50:57.030
essentially.

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:59.680
Like, you have to let the
users tell you what to do.

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:02.543
And I think that that
is extremely hard.

00:51:02.543 --> 00:51:05.680
[LAUGHS] Getting
feedback is hard.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:08.838
And definitely not
something that people

00:51:08.838 --> 00:51:09.880
are trained to do, right?

00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:11.160
No, yeah.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:12.480
And then I feel like--

00:51:12.480 --> 00:51:13.710
I mean, I don't know.

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:17.540
Maybe it's just curmudgeonly,
because I see it the most.

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:20.670
But I feel like,
especially in academic sets

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:23.350
and in data science
in general, people

00:51:23.350 --> 00:51:26.070
do take it really personally.

00:51:26.070 --> 00:51:27.990
There's a quote
that I kind of hate

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:31.500
where it's like, photographers
and statisticians both fall

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:32.610
in love with their models.

00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:35.991
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:37.440 --> 00:51:38.925
I feel bad rep--

00:51:38.925 --> 00:51:40.992
but it makes the
point that like--

00:51:40.992 --> 00:51:42.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:45.390
You see people like
really dig their heels in.

00:51:45.390 --> 00:51:47.880
And it gets personal.

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:50.920
And what I like is that
at least with the user--

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:52.420
I think in the
design field, there's

00:51:52.420 --> 00:51:57.210
a lot more focus on
that accepting feedback.

00:51:57.210 --> 00:51:59.860
And then also I mean, another
thing kind of personally--

00:51:59.860 --> 00:52:04.050
Hilary's personal corner--
of the one thing I

00:52:04.050 --> 00:52:07.320
did not expect from starting
this kind of meditation

00:52:07.320 --> 00:52:11.400
practice and going down that
path is that, it genuinely

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:14.605
made me better at getting
empathy and getting feedback.

00:52:14.605 --> 00:52:16.230
Because essentially
the whole practice,

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:19.710
the whole idea is like
dissociating yourself

00:52:19.710 --> 00:52:21.090
from who you think you are.

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:25.080
So it allows you to not
feel personally threatened

00:52:25.080 --> 00:52:28.145
if someone challenged--

00:52:28.145 --> 00:52:29.520
People take stuff
personally when

00:52:29.520 --> 00:52:31.978
it's like your whole identity
is that you're a statistician

00:52:31.978 --> 00:52:33.270
and you're smart, right?

00:52:33.270 --> 00:52:36.570
And then if someone says
your model is wrong,

00:52:36.570 --> 00:52:38.700
it's like, oh, no,
I'm not smart anymore.

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:40.290
My whole identity
is, like, gone.

00:52:40.290 --> 00:52:42.690
And then you're defensive.

00:52:42.690 --> 00:52:47.430
And so figuring out ways--
and for me, like meditation--

00:52:47.430 --> 00:52:51.660
but just figuring out ways
to detach yourself from that

00:52:51.660 --> 00:52:53.730
makes you able to take feedback.

00:52:53.730 --> 00:52:56.250
And then that actually makes
you better at the thing.

00:52:56.250 --> 00:52:58.020
So it's kind of like a paradox.

00:52:58.020 --> 00:52:59.520
But it's not like
a magical thing.

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:01.062
It's something that
can be practiced.

00:53:01.062 --> 00:53:01.650
Exactly.

00:53:01.650 --> 00:53:04.980
So yeah, the thing about
the meditation practice

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:06.943
that was a big
paradigm shift for me

00:53:06.943 --> 00:53:09.360
was that these things aren't
just, like, fixed properties.

00:53:09.360 --> 00:53:11.190
It's not just like,
oh, this person's

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:13.530
good at getting feedback or
this person has a thick skin

00:53:13.530 --> 00:53:17.430
and that's a set character
trait and mine is not

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:18.270
and that's done.

00:53:18.270 --> 00:53:22.380
It's like, no, there are
ways to essentially engage

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:24.850
in practice that makes
you more robust to that.

00:53:24.850 --> 00:53:29.550
And again, looking at kind of
the neuroscience and Buddhism

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:33.840
stuff, you have
neuroplasticity and you

00:53:33.840 --> 00:53:37.680
can make new neural pathways
that make this stuff--

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:40.860
it's not just like,
oh, yeah, I swear.

00:53:40.860 --> 00:53:43.790
There are ways that you can
essentially change your brain.

00:53:43.790 --> 00:53:48.450
And actually the Nigel Cross
stuff goes into that, of like,

00:53:48.450 --> 00:53:50.250
you look at people
who are cab drivers

00:53:50.250 --> 00:53:52.530
and their spatial
regions of their brains

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.360
are much more developed.

00:53:54.360 --> 00:53:59.610
So yeah, doing
creative work is hard.

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:02.620
And it's very different
than doing scientific work.

00:54:02.620 --> 00:54:05.620
And it's also what we're doing.

00:54:05.620 --> 00:54:07.680
And there are ways
to get good at it.

00:54:07.680 --> 00:54:08.980
There's ways to practice it.

00:54:08.980 --> 00:54:11.040
There's ways to get good.

00:54:11.040 --> 00:54:12.510
Doing this whole podcast--

00:54:12.510 --> 00:54:17.100
it's like being OK with
iterating and showing

00:54:17.100 --> 00:54:21.870
things that will be wrong
in the future, it's OK.

00:54:21.870 --> 00:54:24.475
And actually, it'll
make you better.

00:54:24.475 --> 00:54:26.100
And that's kind of
why I feel like I've

00:54:26.100 --> 00:54:28.650
wanted to talk so much
about the Design Thinking

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:33.060
is because I want people
to feel empowered to engage

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:37.730
in that part of
honing their craft

00:54:37.730 --> 00:54:39.750
and not feeling
intimidated by it.

00:54:39.750 --> 00:54:41.640
I think probably a lot
of people in this room

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:43.380
don't think they're creative.

00:54:43.380 --> 00:54:44.750
I didn't think I was creative.

00:54:44.750 --> 00:54:47.530
And I don't think that I--

00:54:47.530 --> 00:54:48.820
yeah, it's just like identity.

00:54:48.820 --> 00:54:50.020
It's, like, I'm a scientist.

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:51.660
Hilary does science--
math and science.

00:54:51.660 --> 00:54:55.330
And I want people to go through
that same process I went

00:54:55.330 --> 00:54:57.330
through of, like, opening
up that side of myself

00:54:57.330 --> 00:54:59.980
and being like,
oh, this stuff all

00:54:59.980 --> 00:55:03.700
makes me better at this work and
it's more fun, because of it.

00:55:03.700 --> 00:55:05.040
And you can do it, too.

00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:06.507
Like, anyone can do it.

00:55:06.507 --> 00:55:07.401
[LAUGHS]

00:55:07.401 --> 00:55:08.250
So anyway.

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:10.100
That was, like, sitcom ending.

00:55:10.100 --> 00:55:10.740
There you go.

00:55:10.740 --> 00:55:12.060
Except that we do
have one piece--

00:55:12.060 --> 00:55:12.570
We have one more.

00:55:12.570 --> 00:55:14.570
Yeah, so that was, like,
bigger recurring theme.

00:55:14.570 --> 00:55:17.860
We have one more recurring
theme that we are not

00:55:17.860 --> 00:55:18.875
open to feedback on.

00:55:18.875 --> 00:55:19.500
That's right.

00:55:19.500 --> 00:55:21.240
So we have one piece of--

00:55:21.240 --> 00:55:22.830
this is the only
data in our whole--

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:23.850
Yeah.

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:26.130
You did make this in R, right?

00:55:26.130 --> 00:55:27.900
So we do have R in this talk.

00:55:27.900 --> 00:55:29.020
Oh, there is R, yeah.

00:55:29.020 --> 00:55:29.520
Yeah.

00:55:29.760 --> 00:55:30.968
This is a ggplot, actually.

00:55:30.968 --> 00:55:32.020
So we have one more clip.

00:55:33.565 --> 00:55:35.690
ROGER PENG (RECORDING):
Now we're bringing tea time

00:55:35.690 --> 00:55:36.980
